= Results {#ch:results}

== Density estimates

@tab:errors compares the root mean square error\ix{RMSE} of the density estimates
against the reference densities of the validation detectors. Adding floating-car speeds
reduces the error by 31% overall, and by almost half in congestion, where detector data
alone react late to the queue tail.

\caption[Table, id=tab:errors]{Density error (RMSE, veh/km) by traffic condition}
\table[style="Thesis Table", header-rows=1, widths=(5cm, 3cm, 3cm, 2.5cm), columns=(left, decimal, decimal, decimal)]{
  | Condition   | Detectors | Detectors + FCD | Change |
  | Free flow   | 4.1       | 3.6             | -12%   |
  | Transition  | 9.8       | 6.9             | -30%   |
  | Congestion  | 17.2      | 9.1             | -47%   |
  | \cell[colspan=1]{*All*} | *8.6* | *5.9*    | *-31%* |
}

== Stop-and-go waves

@fig:speed shows the estimated speeds on the morning of the first day. A stop-and-go wave
starts near the downstream end at 7:40 and travels upstream at about 18 km/h, the wave
speed of @tab:fd, as kinematic wave theory predicts \cite[page=89]{treiber2013}.

\figure[id=fig:speed, width=13cm]{
  \image[alt="Speed by time and position: a red band runs diagonally from the lower right to the upper left"]{../img/speed-field.png}
  \caption{Estimated speeds, 7:00 to 9:00; red is slow, green is free flow}
}

The filter places the wave front within one cell (500 m) of the position seen in the
floating-car data in 34 of the 37 logged incidents.
